\title{multisiteDGP: Simulate summary data for multisite trials}
\description{
Simulates the summary data of a multisite trial: one estimated effect and
one standard error per site. Site effects come from one of six distributions
or from a user-supplied function. Standard errors are derived from simulated
site sizes or set directly, and can be correlated with the site effects.
}
\details{
A simulation has four steps: site effects (\code{\link[=gen_effects]{gen_effects()}}), site sizes
and standard errors (\code{\link[=gen_site_sizes]{gen_site_sizes()}}), an optional dependence between
effects and precision (\code{\link[=align_rank_corr]{align_rank_corr()}}, \code{\link[=align_copula_corr]{align_copula_corr()}}, or
\code{\link[=align_hybrid_corr]{align_hybrid_corr()}}), and observed estimates (\code{\link[=gen_observations]{gen_observations()}}).
\code{\link[=sim_multisite]{sim_multisite()}} runs the four steps in one call. \code{\link[=sim_meta]{sim_meta()}} does the
same, with the standard errors set directly by \code{\link[=gen_se_direct]{gen_se_direct()}}.

To start, read \code{vignette("multisiteDGP")} and run \code{\link[=sim_multisite]{sim_multisite()}} with
one of the \link{presets}.
}
\section{Seeds}{

With a \code{seed}, \code{\link[=sim_multisite]{sim_multisite()}} and \code{\link[=sim_meta]{sim_meta()}} set the random number
generator to Mersenne-Twister with inversion and rejection sampling, run
the simulation, and then put the generator of your session back as it
was. The same seed gives the same data in any session. Without a seed
they draw from the generator of your session.
}

\section{Errors}{

Every error that the package signals has class \code{multisitedgp_error}. An
error from a numerical procedure that could not reach its target also
has class \code{multisitedgp_solver_error}.
}

